          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "Preventer inhalers are hard to keep up because they do nothing you can feel. The asthma adherence guidance in the corpus notes that the daily inhaler \"produces no sensation,\" while a reliever gives immediate relief. So when someone feels well, the daily inhaler is the one that gets dropped first, and that is exactly when it is working. (Source: *Sticking with asthma treatment*, lifestyle-asthma-adherence.)\n\n**Technique problems can also undermine it.** The same document says poor inhaler technique is common and can persist for years. It can mean a person takes the inhaler every day but gets only a fraction of the dose. Technique is usually checked before a treatment is judged to have failed. Pressurized inhalers and dry powder inhalers need opposite breathing styles, so someone using both types may find them confusing.\n\n**Routine helps.** The diabetes adherence guidance in the corpus suggests tying doses to an existing daily habit rather than a clock time, and using reminders. The corpus is about diabetes, but the same idea applies here. Keeping a written action plan where you can find it, on your phone or with family, also helps. (Source: lifestyle-asthma-adherence.)\n\n**Side effects:** The corpus does not describe side effects of preventer inhalers, so I can't say what they are or how common they are. If you have concerns, a clinician or pharmacist can explain them and check your technique.\n\n**Practical points from the guidance:**\n- Confirm which inhaler is the daily one and which is the reliever.\n- Ask a clinician to check your technique at your next review.\n- Tell your clinician if you are missing doses, so the plan can be reviewed rather than quietly dropped.\n- If you find yourself using the reliever more often, the guidance treats that as a sign the asthma may not be controlled. Speak to a clinician promptly about it.\n\nIf you have sudden or severe breathlessness, trouble speaking, or blue lips, seek emergency help straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
